= Holobiome =

== Overview ==
'''Holobiome''' is a biological and ecological term used for collective host-associated or interconnected biological communities, especially when emphasizing microorganisms and their relationships with larger hosts or environments.

Unlike [[Holobiont|holobiont]], the term does not yet have one consistently applied technical meaning across the literature. Some authors use ''holobiome'' for the collective biotic or genomic complement associated with a holobiont; others use it more broadly for interacting microbiomes and hosts across an ecosystem or metacommunity.

For BITwiki, an occurrence of ''holobiome'' should therefore be accompanied by an explicit definition of what organisms, genomes, spatial scale, and environmental compartments are included.

== Relationship to holobiont ==
A '''holobiont''' normally centers a host together with its associated symbionts.

A '''holobiome''' may instead emphasize the collective biological community or extend the analysis across multiple interacting host-associated and environmental microbiomes.

That distinction is useful, but it is not standardized strongly enough to infer scope from the word alone.

== Relationship to hologenome ==
''Hologenome'' usually refers more specifically to the collective genetic information of a host plus its associated microorganisms.

Some literature has used ''holobiome'' and ''hologenome'' in overlapping ways, while newer ecological usage sometimes applies ''holobiome'' to broader networks spanning hosts and environments.

Accordingly:

<pre>
holobiont   → organisms considered together
hologenome  → their collective genetic information
holobiome   → variable usage; often the broader associated biotic/microbial system
</pre>

The definitions used by a particular study take precedence over this shorthand.

== Systems perspective ==
Holobiome research can connect host-associated microbiomes with environmental reservoirs, dispersal, metabolism, disease ecology, agriculture, and ecosystem processes.

A systems treatment should identify:

* the focal hosts and microbial communities;
* spatial and temporal boundaries;
* routes of microbial dispersal;
* interaction types;
* environmental inputs;
* measured functions rather than taxonomic association alone.

This prevents a broad ecological metaphor from being mistaken for evidence of functional integration.

== Epistemic caution ==
Claims that a holobiome behaves as a coherent evolutionary individual, possesses a stable collective genome, or has a unified function require additional evidence. Microbial partners can be transient, horizontally acquired, antagonistic, or environmentally determined.

The term is therefore useful when its boundary is operationally specified, not as a universal assertion that all associated organisms form one organism-like unit.

